  "body": {
    "id": "prop:joint-column-singleton-criterion",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "For every fixed instance covered by \\(\\text{thm:deterministic-joint-effect-image}\\), the joint set \\(S_{\\bullet u}\\) is a singleton if and only if either \\(g_{\\mathcal G,\\ell}(u)=\\bot\\), or \\(g_{\\mathcal G,\\ell}(u)=g\\in[k]\\) and one of the following holds: \\(L_u=\\varnothing\\); \\(z_u=1\\) and \\(W_{vc}=0\\) for every \\(c\\in L_u\\) and every \\(v\\ne u\\); or \\(z_u=0\\), \\(L_u\\ne\\varnothing\\), and\n\\[\nW_{vc}W_{ud}-W_{vd}W_{uc}=0\n\\]\nfor every \\(v\\in V\\) and every \\(c,d\\in L_u\\).  The common vector is \\(e_u\\) in the first two cogent cases and any normalized column \\(W_{\\bullet c}/W_{uc}\\) in the last case.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "W",
      "Ggrp",
      "ell",
      "Scolu",
      "u",
      "v",
      "V",
      "gmap",
      "g",
      "k",
      "Lu",
      "zu",
      "c",
      "d",
      "eu"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:effect-image",
      "thm:deterministic-joint-effect-image"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "The fixed-mleaf branch is \\(\\{e_u\\}\\). Suppose \\(g_{\\mathcal G,\\ell}(u)=g\\in[k]\\). Every \\(c\\in L_u\\) occurs as a legal pivot with center \\(u\\), so \\(W_{uc}\\ne0\\). If \\(L_u=\\varnothing\\), nonemptiness of \\(P_g\\) forces an attaining pair with center different from \\(u\\), whence \\(z_u=1\\) and \\(S_{\\bullet u}=\\{e_u\\}\\).\n\nIf \\(z_u=1\\), then \\(e_u\\in S_{\\bullet u}\\). A normalized column equals \\(e_u\\) exactly when, for every \\(v\\ne u\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{W_{vc}}{W_{uc}}=0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad W_{vc}=0.\n\\]\nThus the joint set is a singleton exactly under the stated zero tests, and its value is \\(e_u\\).\n\nIf \\(z_u=0\\) and \\(L_u\\ne\\varnothing\\), no \\(e_u\\) is adjoined, so singletonness is equality of all normalized column vectors. For \\(c,d\\in L_u\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{W_{vc}}{W_{uc}}=\\frac{W_{vd}}{W_{ud}}\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nW_{vc}W_{ud}-W_{vd}W_{uc}=0,\n\\]\nbecause both denominators are nonzero. Requiring this for every \\(v\\in V\\) is equivalent to vector equality. These cases are exhaustive and prove both directions and the common values.",
